10. SECURITY ADVISORY — VALIDATED & HARDENED (v3.0)

Local does not automatically mean secure. Zigbee pairing can leak encryption keys. IPv6 introduces attack surfaces for Thread and Matter.

10.1 Zigbee Risks & Mitigations

· Default insecure key: Many installations use the well‑known key 00000000000000000000000000000000.
    Fix (v3.0 mandatory): In zigbee2mqtt/configuration.yaml, set network_key: GENERATE before pairing any device. Re‑pair all existing devices.
· CVE‑2021‑27289 (Replay attack): An attacker within wireless range can replay captured packets with higher sequence numbers.
    Mitigation: Never leave Permit Join open, use secure commissioning, and update coordinator firmware regularly.

10.2 IPv6 & Thread Risks (Anticipated)

· Constrained device IPv6 stacks may have vulnerabilities like Router Advertisement handling flaws.
    Mitigation: Keep coordinator firmware updated. Use a dedicated VLAN for IoT devices. Restrict physical access to border routers.

10.3 Defense in Depth

· Isolate all smart home devices on a separate VLAN.
· Regularly update coordinator firmware (but freeze to known‑good builds for 90 days).
· Use WireGuard VPN for remote access; never open ports directly.
· Deploy the physical mute button to guarantee no accidental voice recording.

